//# TWO ENUMS EACH DECLARING A STRUCT-SHAPED VARIANT OF THE SAME NAME, with
//# DIFFERENT field defaults and a DIFFERENT required-field set.
//#
//# This fixture was UNWRITABLE until 2026-08-25 and that is the reason it
//# exists. The interpreter keyed a struct-shaped variant's synthetic
//# declaration by the VARIANT NAME ALONE, so `Signal.Go` and `Gear.Go` shared
//# ONE registration and the later declaration won: `Signal.Go{}.n` answered 7
//# where 3 is correct. A differential over this shape would have compared a
//# correct builder against a wrong reference and reported the BUILDER as the
//# diff, which is why dot_variant.nomi carried the pair with disjoint names and
//# a note instead. Both sides agree now.
//#
//# WHAT EACH LINE DISCRIMINATES, because a fixture that only printed defaults
//# would pass against a registration keyed either way once the counts differ:
//#   - `Signal.Go{}` and `Gear.Go{}` take DIFFERENT defaults for the SAME field
//#     name. A shared registration answers one number twice.
//#   - `Signal.Go{n: 5}` proves an explicit value still wins, so the fix did not
//#     turn the default path into an override.
//#   - `Gear.Go{label: "x"}` has a field `Signal.Go` does not, and `Signal.Go`
//#     requires nothing extra — so a shared registration would raise a spurious
//#     `missing field` error for one of the two, which is the half of this
//#     defect that fails LOUDLY rather than silently.
//#   - The `Halt`/`Park` bare variants keep both enums multi-variant, so the
//#     expected type is never recoverable from the variant name alone.
//#
//# ORDER MATTERS: `Gear` is declared SECOND, so under the old keying it was the
//# winner and every reading came back 7. A fixture declaring the wanted answer
//# second would have passed against the defect.

import std/io

enum Signal {
    Go {n: Int = 3}
    Halt
}

enum Gear {
    Go {n: Int = 7, label: String}
    Park
}

fn main() {
    first = Signal.Go{}
    second = Gear.Go{label: "top"}
    explicit = Signal.Go{n: 5}

    io.print("signal-default ${first.n}")
    io.print("gear-default ${second.n}")
    io.print("gear-label ${second.label}")
    io.print("signal-explicit ${explicit.n}")
}
